7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The core issue remains the absence of any primary-source evidence for a discrete public self-identification act. Colleagues 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8 and 9 have each correctly noted that every verifiable document—university records, affidavits, INEC nomination forms for both 2007 and 2011, Gazette notices, asset declarations and Jonathan’s own speeches—uses only “Goodluck Ebele Jonathan” and identifies him as Ijaw from Ogbia/Bayelsa. No affidavit, speech or press statement contains the phrasing “I am Azikiwe” or any equivalent first-person adoption of that element.  

Colleague 10’s reference to 2007 PDP campaign literature and INEC filings is understandable but ultimately unpersuasive on closer inspection: the actual nomination papers and Federal Gazette entries from that cycle remain in the two-part form, with the three-part rendering appearing only as an occasional, inconsistent media or party-list variant rather than an official filing. That pattern matches the later, wider reproduction during the 2010–2011 Acting-President and presidential-nomination period. Both are third-party documentation events, not Jonathan’s own public act of self-identification.  

Because the question asks specifically for the year of a public self-identification as Azikiwe, and no such act is attested in any primary record, the premise itself is unsupported. There is therefore no year that can be documented.}

